---
import CaseStudy from "@/layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import QuickEntryDemo from "@/components/QuickEntryDemo.astro";
import { projects } from "@/data/projects";

const project = projects.find((p) => p.slug === "ambry-calendar")!;
---

<CaseStudy project={project}>
  <section class="case-block" aria-labelledby="try-title">
    <h2 id="try-title" data-reveal>Try it</h2>
    <div data-reveal>
      <p class="case-lede" style="margin-bottom: 28px;">
        The whole product hangs on one interaction. Here it is, running the same parser the app uses.
      </p>
      <QuickEntryDemo />
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="prose-case" data-reveal>
    <h2>Why a grid</h2>
    <p>
      Ambry started as a spreadsheet: rows for days, columns for the parts of a life — school, practice, appointments,
      personal. It worked better than any calendar app at the one thing calendars are supposed to do, which is showing
      you an open day at a glance. But a spreadsheet doesn't sync, doesn't remind, and doesn't talk to a real calendar.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the grid became the product, and every cell became an input. A typed line is a real, timed event the moment
      you press return. Color carries status — confirmed, tentative, not going — rather than category, and a cancelled
      entry is struck through instead of deleted, so the history stays honest.
    </p>

    <h2>Native, on purpose</h2>
    <p>
      The Mac and iPhone apps are SwiftUI rather than a wrapped website, because the details that make a calendar feel
      fast — real pinch-to-zoom on the grid, trackpad panning, keyboard switching between views, system Face ID lock —
      only exist natively. A full React web app talks to the same backend, so nothing is lost by opening it in a
      browser instead.
    </p>

    <h2>Shipping in public</h2>
    <p>
      Ambry has gone from an empty repository to a native Mac and iPhone app, a web app, and a live marketing site,
      across more than twenty versioned releases — each one logged in a changelog the app itself reads to show what's
      new.
    </p>
  </div>
</CaseStudy>
